  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through next Friday)\nIssued at 1119 PM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nRain chances continue to increase across West Central Texas for\nSaturday and Sunday. Upper level trough over the Southwest US\ntoday will dive south, sharpen, and then briefly close off over \nnorthern Mexico Saturday morning. Upper level jet streak moving\nacross the area, upper level diffluence, and isentropic lift over\nthe frontal boundary will all combine to produce widespread lift\nacross the area. A little bit of drier near surface air across the\narea on Saturday morning will saturate as the shower start to\nmove into the area, with the activity becoming much more\nwidespread Saturday afternoon into Saturday night. CAMs like the\nRRFS show multiple waves of showers and a few storms possible,\nSaturday Afternoon and evening, Sunday morning, and a third late\nSunday night into Monday morning. Models never end up getting\nthese waves and timing right, since they are heavily influenced by\nmesoscale processes of each event. Still though, rain chances look\ngood into Sunday afternoon and Sunday Night.\n\nThe RRFS does spread rain across much of West Central Texas for\nthis event, while some of the other models like the GFS and the\nmodel blends show a little more concentrated area along I-10 and\nless precipitation along and north of I-20. Given the placement of\nthe old frontal boundary which would tend to hold the more\nwidespread rain to the south like the GFS and blends, not going \nto argue with them and will continue the idea of highest POP\nchances south and fairly significant drop off north of I-20.\n\nGiven the cloud cover on Saturday, did lower highs a few degrees\nto better match some of the low level temperature fields from the\nHRRR and RRFS. \n\nSystem moves away from the area for early next week, leaving most\nof next week dry. After the cool weekend with highs in the\n50s/60s, afternoon highs will rebound back into the 70s for much\nof next week. Lows will drop into the 40s and 50s.",
